[hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:]
[D] If you were given a choice between a playing a game where you could
[D] potentially get 7 wishes at a time and numerous wands of polymorph to
[D]do unthinkable things with or playing a game where wishes were rare
[D] and polymorph wasn't such an easy out which would you choose (honestly)?

[c164-ac@yorric.uucp (Tom Holub) writes:]
[T] The latter, in a second.  That's why I play Moria a lot more than NetHack,
[T] because there's so little good luck and so much reamage :)  I mean, in any
[T] of these games you have a huge advantage over any of the creatures in the
[T] dungeon, why bother exacerbating it by granting wishes all the time?  
[T] Gimme a tough game, anyday.

[an excerpt from what cgoret@nmsu.edu (Clay Goret) writes:]
[C] I find it *HIGHLY* annoying when I get to the 31 level, after fighting
[C] my way through a horde of nasties, and find a wand of wishing with ONE
[C] charge.  Why ONE charge?  Has someone else used it (the wizard perhaps?)
[C] After killing of the inhabitants of the dungeon as far as this level, I
[C] am usually in poor shape to battle those damn minotaurs (who seem more
[C] than able to strip me of 40 or so hit points per attack!).  That wand is
[C] usually the difference between me being able to continue or not...

and so it goes ... 

I'd like to know how _you_ feel about this. Be creative (i.e. it may be
possible to distribute wishes more uniformly thru the game). Send me mail
if you don't want to post.

